  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs high pressure drifts off the East Coast today, a weak\ndisturbance is expected to move north into the Ohio Valley,\nbringing the potential for showers across the far eastern CWA.\nThese showers should remain isolated to scattered given the\noverall weak forcing with the best timing being this afternoon\ninto the evening hours. At the same time, a cold front will very\nslowly be approaching the area from the west. This cold front,\nassociated with a broad upper level trough and surface low over\nnorthern Canada, is expected to become nearly stationary just\nwest of the CWA which will keep the entire area within the warm\nsector of the low for Thursday and much of Friday. This will \nallow for a consistent southwest flow and increased moisture \nadvection into the Ohio Valley which will allow for dew point\ntemperatures to increase into the upper 60s to low 70s. Initially\non Thursday, the area may be split between widespread showers \nand thunderstorms going on to the east and to the west of the \nCWA with the greatest chance of showers across the far western \nand eastern fringes of the CWA. In any areas that receive\nshowers, heavy rainfall and localized flooding will be possible\ngiven the PWAT values over 1.5 inches. In addition, the severe\nweather potential looks to be minimal on Thursday given the weak\nenvironment. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front will begin to move across the \narea on Friday, increasing the likelihood for widespread showers\nand thunderstorms across the area. With dewpoints still \nlingering in the upper 60s to low 70s and high PWAT values, \nheavy rainfall will be the primary concern. In addition, very \nslow storm motion and general flow parallel to the boundary may \nresult in training showers and increased flooding concerns in \nany areas impacted. Overall organized convection seems unlikely \ngiven the overall weak mesoscale environment, but cannot rule \nout a few more developed storms given a bit more shear present. \nWPC has highlighted the area in an ERO due to the heavy rain \npotential on Friday. \n\nThis cold front will gradually shift east of the area Saturday\nwhich should allow for a break in precipitation at some point\nthis weekend. There remains quite a bit of uncertainty about\nwhen that break will occur, but with this update the best chance\nis early Sunday before another system impacts the area late\nSunday. This active pattern is expected to continue into next\nweek. Temperatures throughout the entire period will be near\naverage with highs in the 80s and overnight lows in the 60s.",
